<article class="env-lemma" id="0FW4">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0FW4">Lemma <span data-tag="0FW4">50.20.1</span>.</a> Let $k$ be a field. Let $X$ be a nonempty smooth proper scheme over $k$ equidimensional of dimension $d$. There exists a $k$-linear map </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  t : H^ d(X, \Omega ^ d_{X/k}) \longrightarrow k  \]
</div>
  <p> unique up to precomposing by multiplication by a unit of $H^0(X, \mathcal{O}_ X)$ with the following property: for all $p, q$ the pairing </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  H^ q(X, \Omega ^ p_{X/k}) \times H^{d - q}(X, \Omega ^{d - p}_{X/k}) \longrightarrow k, \quad (\xi , \xi ') \longmapsto t(\xi \cup \xi ')  \]
</div>
  <p> is perfect. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      By Duality for Schemes, Lemma <a href="/tag/0FVV" data-tag="0FVV">48.27.1</a> we have $\omega _ X^\bullet = \Omega ^ d_{X/k}[d]$. Since $\Omega _{X/k}$ is locally free of rank $d$ (Morphisms, Lemma <a href="/tag/02G1" data-tag="02G1">29.35.12</a>) we have 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  \Omega ^ d_{X/k} \otimes _{\mathcal{O}_ X} (\Omega ^ p_{X/k})^\vee \cong \Omega ^{d - p}_{X/k}  \]
</div>
    <p>
       Thus we obtain a $k$-linear map $t : H^ d(X, \Omega ^ d_{X/k}) \to k$ such that the statement is true by Duality for Schemes, Lemma <a href="/tag/0FVY" data-tag="0FVY">48.27.4</a>. In particular the pairing $H^0(X, \mathcal{O}_ X) \times H^ d(X, \Omega ^ d_{X/k}) \to k$ is perfect, which implies that any $k$-linear map $t' : H^ d(X, \Omega ^ d_{X/k}) \to k$ is of the form $\xi \mapsto t(g\xi )$ for some $g \in H^0(X, \mathcal{O}_ X)$. Of course, in order for $t'$ to still produce a duality between $H^0(X, \mathcal{O}_ X)$ and $H^ d(X, \Omega ^ d_{X/k})$ we need $g$ to be a unit. Denote $\langle -, - \rangle _{p, q}$ the pairing constructed using $t$ and denote $\langle -, - \rangle '_{p, q}$ the pairing constructed using $t'$. Clearly we have 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  \langle \xi , \xi ' \rangle '_{p, q} = \langle g\xi , \xi ' \rangle _{p, q}  \]
</div>
    <p>
       for $\xi \in H^ q(X, \Omega ^ p_{X/k})$ and $\xi ' \in H^{d - q}(X, \Omega ^{d - p}_{X/k})$. Since $g$ is a unit, i.e., invertible, we see that using $t'$ instead of $t$ we still get perfect pairings for all $p, q$. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>